{"subjectId":"python","id":"comprehensions","title":"Comprehensions","group":"Data Structures","order":18,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Comprehensions build lists, dicts and sets in one readable expression: <em>what</em> to produce, <em>from where</em>, and <em>which</em> items.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>The shape</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>squares = [x * x for x in range(6)]\nevens = [x for x in range(10) if x % 2 == 0]\nlabels = [\"even\" if x % 2 == 0 else \"odd\" for x in range(4)]\nprint(squares)    # [0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25]\nprint(evens)      # [0, 2, 4, 6, 8]\nprint(labels)     # ['even', 'odd', 'even', 'odd']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A filter <code>if</code> goes <strong>after</strong> the <code>for</code>. A conditional <em>value</em> (<code>a if c else b</code>) goes <strong>before</strong> it.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-braces\"></i>Dict &amp; set comprehensions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>words = [\"apple\", \"bat\", \"cherry\"]\nlengths = {w: len(w) for w in words}\ninverted = {v: k for k, v in lengths.items()}\nfirst_letters = {w[0] for w in words}\nprint(lengths)            # {'apple': 5, 'bat': 3, 'cherry': 6}\nprint(inverted[3])        # bat\nprint(sorted(first_letters))   # ['a', 'b', 'c']</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>Nested loops &amp; flattening</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>matrix = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nflat = [x for row in matrix for x in row]          # outer loop first\ntransposed = [list(col) for col in zip(*matrix)]\npairs = [(i, j) for i in range(3) for j in range(i + 1, 3)]\nprint(flat)          # [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\nprint(transposed)    # [[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]]\nprint(pairs)         # [(0, 1), (0, 2), (1, 2)]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Generator expressions</h3>\n<p>Swap <code>[]</code> for <code>()</code> and nothing is built up front — values are produced lazily, one at a time. Ideal when you only aggregate.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>total = sum(x * x for x in range(1_000_000))   # no million-item list in memory\nprint(total)                                    # 333332833333500000\nprint(any(n &gt; 10 for n in [3, 12, 5]))          # True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use comprehensions for simple map/filter transformations that fit on one or two lines.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Nesting three levels deep or adding side effects (<code>[print(x) for x in xs]</code>) — write a normal loop instead.</p></div></div></section>"}